      <h1>Write Clear and Concise Website Copy</h1>
      <p class="intro">Help visitors understand what you offer, decide whether it fits their needs, and take the next step with confidence.</p>
    </header>
    <article>
      <p>Clear website copy answers the questions your customers bring to a page. Concise copy does that without making them work through unnecessary words. That does not mean every page should be short. A service page may need details about pricing, the process, and what is included before someone feels ready to contact you.</p>
      <p>The goal is to give readers enough useful information in a form they can understand. Use the steps and examples below to improve a homepage, service page, or contact page. The sample businesses and offers are illustrative; only use claims that are accurate for your business.</p>

      <section>
        <h2>Write to one reader and one immediate need</h2>
        <p>Before drafting, picture the person visiting the page. What happened that made them look for help? What do they already know? What could stop them from choosing a provider? A homeowner with a leaking pipe needs different information from someone planning a bathroom renovation.</p>
        <p>Write down the reader's main question in their words. For example: “Can someone repair my water heater, and how do I find out what it will cost?” That question gives the page a useful starting point.</p>
        <div class="worksheet">
          <h3>Example: a service-page introduction</h3>
          <p><strong>Before:</strong> “We provide comprehensive plumbing solutions tailored to meet the diverse needs of our valued customers.”</p>
          <p><strong>After:</strong> “Water heater not working? We repair and replace residential water heaters in Charleston. Call to describe the problem and ask about available appointments.”</p>
          <p><strong>Why it is clearer:</strong> The reader can identify the problem, the service, the area, and the next step without translating business language.</p>
        </div>
        <p>Use terms your customers recognize. If a technical term matters, explain it where it appears. “Responsive design” becomes more useful when you explain that the layout adjusts to phones, tablets, and desktop screens.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Give the page one primary job</h2>
        <p>Decide what someone should understand or do after reading. A repair page might help visitors request an appointment. A comparison article might help them choose between two options. Several related questions can belong on one page, but they should support the same purpose.</p>
        <p>Use this simple outline for a service page:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>What you offer:</strong> Name the service and who it is for.</li>
          <li><strong>When it helps:</strong> Describe the problems or situations it addresses.</li>
          <li><strong>What is included:</strong> Explain the work and any important limits.</li>
          <li><strong>What to expect:</strong> Describe the process, timing, and how pricing is determined.</li>
          <li><strong>What to do next:</strong> Give one clear primary action.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Move unrelated detail to a separate page and link to it with descriptive words. “See our website design process” tells readers more than “click here.” Keep essential decision-making information on the page instead of making visitors search for it.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Make headlines specific and sections easy to scan</h2>
        <p>A headline should help readers recognize that they are in the right place. A clever phrase can add personality, but it should not hide the offer. Use section headings that explain what follows, then keep each paragraph focused on one idea.</p>
        <div class="worksheet">
          <h3>Example: a homepage headline</h3>
          <p><strong>Before:</strong> “Taking Your Business to the Next Level”</p>
          <p><strong>After:</strong> “Website Design for Local Service Businesses”</p>
          <p><strong>Supporting copy:</strong> “Explain your services, show examples of your work, and make it easy for customers to request a quote.”</p>
          <p><strong>Why it is clearer:</strong> The headline names the service and audience. The supporting sentence explains what the website should help visitors do.</p>
        </div>
        <p>Replace headings such as “Our Solutions” with useful labels such as “What Your Website Project Includes.” Use numbered lists for steps and bullets for items readers need to compare. Check the page on a phone: a paragraph that looks manageable on a laptop can become a wall of text on a small screen.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Replace vague promises with useful details</h2>
        <p>Words such as “innovative,” “premium,” and “results-driven” do not explain what a customer will receive. Look for places where you can describe the work, show an example, or explain a policy. Specific information helps people evaluate an offer.</p>
        <div class="worksheet">
          <h3>Example: describing a website project</h3>
          <p><strong>Before:</strong> “We deliver cutting-edge websites and unmatched customer service.”</p>
          <p><strong>After:</strong> “Your project includes a homepage, service pages, and a contact form. You review the page layouts before development begins, and we show you how to update the finished site.”</p>
          <p><strong>Why it is clearer:</strong> The revised version explains the deliverables and process. Use this wording only if those items are actually included.</p>
        </div>
        <p>Support claims with relevant evidence, such as a real project example or a clearly explained guarantee. Do not invent numbers, testimonials, qualifications, or deadlines to make copy sound stronger. If pricing depends on the scope, explain which factors affect it rather than implying that every job costs the same.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Cut extra words without cutting necessary meaning</h2>
        <p>Edit sentences that delay the point. Prefer familiar words and direct verbs, while keeping conditions and limits that matter to the customer.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>“In order to request an estimate”</strong> becomes <strong>“To request an estimate.”</strong></li>
          <li><strong>“We are able to provide assistance with”</strong> becomes <strong>“We can help with.”</strong></li>
          <li><strong>“An inspection will be performed by our technician”</strong> becomes <strong>“Our technician will inspect the system.”</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Do not shorten a sentence in a way that changes the promise. “Same-day appointments may be available; call to check” should not become “Same-day service guaranteed.” The shorter version introduces a commitment the business may not be able to meet.</p>
        <p>Vary sentence length so the writing sounds natural. Concise copy can still be warm, detailed, and helpful. Remove repetition, not the information a reader needs to make a decision.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Write buttons that explain the next step</h2>
        <p>A call to action should describe what happens when someone clicks. Match the wording to the destination and explain any important expectations nearby.</p>
        <div class="worksheet">
          <h3>Example: a contact button</h3>
          <p><strong>Before:</strong> “Get Started”</p>
          <p><strong>After:</strong> “Request a Website Quote”</p>
          <p><strong>Supporting copy:</strong> “Tell us about your business and the pages you need. We will review your request and discuss the scope with you.”</p>
          <p><strong>Why it is clearer:</strong> Visitors know they are requesting a conversation about a quote, rather than paying for a service or booking a confirmed appointment.</p>
        </div>
        <p>Use “Book an Appointment” only when the next step really lets visitors book. If they are sending an inquiry, “Request an Appointment” is more accurate. Mention a response time only when your team can reliably meet it.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Read the draft aloud and check what readers understand</h2>
        <p>Read the page aloud at a normal pace. Rewrite phrases that make you stumble or sound unlike something you would say to a customer. Then ask someone unfamiliar with the business to read it and explain the offer in their own words.</p>
        <p>Ask specific questions: What does this business do? Who is the service for? What is included? What would you do next? If the answers do not match your intent, revise the unclear section rather than simply adding more words everywhere.</p>
        <p>Try this editing exercise: choose one paragraph, underline the main point, and remove phrases that do not support it. Add back any missing detail about scope, cost, timing, or the next step. Compare the two versions and ask which one makes the decision easier.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="editing-checklist">
        <h2 id="editing-checklist">A practical website copy editing checklist</h2>
        <p>Use this checklist before publishing a new page or updating an existing one.</p>
        <div class="worksheet">
          <h3>Message and accuracy</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Does the opening explain the service and who it is for?</li>
            <li>Does the page answer the reader's main question?</li>
            <li>Are the service area, inclusions, and limitations clear?</li>
            <li>Can you support every claim, number, and promise?</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Structure and wording</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Do the headings explain what each section covers?</li>
            <li>Does each paragraph develop one main idea?</li>
            <li>Have you explained necessary technical terms?</li>
            <li>Have you removed repetition and vague phrases without losing useful detail?</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Next steps and final checks</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Does the main button describe what actually happens next?</li>
            <li>Do links lead to the intended pages and use descriptive text?</li>
            <li>Are names, phone numbers, spelling, and grammar correct?</li>
            <li>Is the page comfortable to read and navigate on a phone?</li>
            <li>Can someone unfamiliar with the business explain the offer after reading?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p>If a section fails a check, fix that specific problem. You do not need to rewrite a useful page from scratch or reach a particular word count.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Start with one page</h2>
        <p>Choose a page customers use to evaluate your business. Clarify its main job, improve one vague headline, add a useful example, and make the next step obvious. Then work through the checklist before moving to another page.</p>
        <p>For help deciding what the page should accomplish, start with your <a href="/resources/blog/defining-your-websites-purpose-and-target-audience/">website purpose and audience</a>. To discuss a website project, <a href="/contact/">contact Holy Webs</a>.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
